AB  - Aiming to expand the knowledge about the occurrence of endophytic fungi associated with cocoa (Theobroma cacao), a study was carried out at the Univesidade Estadual de Santa Cruz (UESC), Ilhéus, Bahia State, during the period from August 2019 to May 2021. Two hundred and seventy isolates were obtained from fragments of leaves and branches collected in the field, considering the rainy and dry periods, and 137 were identified, belonging to nine taxas distributed in nine different families of the Phylum Ascomycota. Selected isolates of Chaetomium globosum, Colletotrichum, Fusarium oxysporum, Neopestalotiopsis and Xylaria were inoculated into seminal seedlings of three cacao varieties under greenhouse conditions, and reisolation was carried out after 60 days. Of the inoculated isolates, all were recovered. Chaetomium globosum and Fusarium oxysporum were most reisolated from the inoculated seedlings, and the first was even present in the non-inoculated seedlings (control), indicating the need to undertake studies that aim to better understand the association of this fungus with the cacao tree, since it was characterized its vertical transmission. The evaluation of the biological control potential of some endophytes found in this study may bring new perspectives for the integrated management of cacao diseases, mainly Witch's Broom (Moniliophthora perniciosa) and Brown Rot (Phytophthora spp.).
